{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"RESEARCH — how actively-maintained Gerrit deployments configure outbound mail on AWS. Commissioned for this bug so the configure-vs-disable decision is grounded in real deployments and primary sources rather than invented. Every claim below carries a source; where nothing could be verified, that is stated explicitly rather than guessed.\n\n# Gerrit outbound mail on AWS — grounded options\n\n**Root cause confirmed against source.** Gerrit's `SmtpEmailSender` defaults `smtpServer` to `127.0.0.1` and, for `smtpEncryption = none|tls`, `smtpServerPort` to `25`. With no `[sendemail]` section at all, Gerrit dials `127.0.0.1:25` on every notification:\n\n    smtpHost = cfg.getString(\"sendemail\", null, \"smtpserver\");\n    if (smtpHost == null) { smtpHost = \"127.0.0.1\"; }\n    ...\n    switch (smtpEncryption) {\n      case SSL: defaultPort = 465; break;\n      case NONE: case TLS: default: defaultPort = 25;\n    }\n\n— https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GerritCodeReview/gerrit/master/java/com/google/gerrit/server/mail/send/SmtpEmailSender.java\n\n**Confirmed: the official image has no MTA, so `localhost:25` can never work.** `GerritCodeReview/docker-gerrit` `ubuntu/26/Dockerfile` installs only `gnupg2`, `sudo`, `openjdk-21-jdk` (then removes it), and `gerrit=3.14.1-1` on `eclipse-temurin` — no postfix/exim/ssmtp/msmtp/nullmailer, no `EXPOSE 25`. Its entrypoint writes exactly two config keys (`gerrit.canonicalWebUrl`, `httpd.listenUrl`) and the README documents exactly two env vars — no SMTP env vars at all.\n— https://github.com/GerritCodeReview/docker-gerrit/blob/master/ubuntu/26/Dockerfile\n— https://github.com/GerritCodeReview/docker-gerrit/blob/master/ubuntu/26/entrypoint.sh\n— https://github.com/GerritCodeReview/docker-gerrit/blob/master/README.md\n\nNote the official README's own production example contains `[sendemail] smtpServer = localhost`, which is misleading for a stock container — it only works if you supply an MTA yourself (see section 2).\n\n## 1. Canonical `[sendemail]` keys\n\n| Key | Accepted values / default | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| `enable` | `true` (default). \"If false Gerrit will not send email messages, for any reason, and all other properties of section sendemail are ignored.\" | config-gerrit; commit 2e4573b464 |\n| `smtpServer` | hostname/IP; **default `127.0.0.1`** | SmtpEmailSender.java |\n| `smtpServerPort` | default `25`, or `465` when `smtpEncryption = ssl` | SmtpEmailSender.java |\n| `smtpEncryption` | **exactly `none` / `ssl` / `tls`** (enum; `tls` = STARTTLS). Default `none`. Typos like `tsl` silently fall through to the default — this bit a real reporter. | SmtpEmailSender.java; repo-discuss |\n| `smtpUser` / `smtpPass` | optional; auth attempted **only if `smtpUser` is non-null** (`if (smtpUser != null && !client.auth(smtpUser, smtpPass))`) | SmtpEmailSender.java |\n| `sslVerify` | default `true` | SmtpEmailSender.java |\n| `from` | default `MIXED`; also `USER`, `SERVER`, or a literal `Name <addr>` with optional `${user}` placeholder | config-gerrit |\n| `connectTimeout`, `includeDiff`, `threadPoolSize`, `allowrcpt`/`denyrcpt`, `threadIdPrefix` | see docs | config-gerrit |\n\n— https://gerrit-review.googlesource.com/Documentation/config-gerrit.html#sendemail\n\n**`smtpPass` belongs in `$site_path/etc/secure.config`, not `gerrit.config`.** `secure.config` \"overrides or supplements the settings supplied by gerrit.config and should be readable only by the daemon process\"; `gerrit init` actively moves `sendemail.smtpPass` into it. Optionally encryptable via the secure-config plugin + `gerrit passwd`.\n— https://gerrit-review.googlesource.com/Documentation/config-gerrit.html\n— https://gerrit.googlesource.com/plugins/secure-config/+/refs/heads/stable-2.13/README.md\n\n**SES port/encryption matrix** (TLS is mandatory; there is no plaintext option):\n\n| Mechanism | SES ports | Gerrit `smtpEncryption` |\n|---|---|---|\n| STARTTLS | 25, 587, 2587 | `tls` |\n| TLS Wrapper (implicit SSL) | 465, 2465 | `ssl` |\n\n— https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ses/latest/dg/smtp-connect.html\n\n## 2. How the big deployments actually do it\n\n**Neither of the two largest public Gerrit instances uses a remote authenticated SMTP server. Both run a local MTA and set `smtpServer = localhost`.**\n\n**OpenDev / OpenStack** — `playbooks/roles/gerrit/templates/gerrit.config.j2`:\n\n    [sendemail]\n        smtpServer = localhost\n        from = MIXED\n        includeDiff = false\n        threadPoolSize = 4\n\nTheir Gerrit container also has no MTA. It works because the compose service runs `network_mode: host`, so `localhost:25` is the *host's* port 25, where the base exim role delivers direct via DNS (no smarthost, no auth). No `smtpUser`/`smtpPass`/`smtpEncryption` anywhere in the role.\n— https://opendev.org/opendev/system-config/src/branch/master/playbooks/roles/gerrit/templates/gerrit.config.j2\n— https://opendev.org/opendev/system-config/src/branch/master/playbooks/roles/gerrit/templates/docker-compose.yaml.j2\n— https://opendev.org/opendev/system-config/src/branch/master/playbooks/roles/base/exim/README.rst\n\n**Wikimedia** — `modules/gerrit/templates/gerrit.config.erb`:\n\n    [sendemail]\n        includeDiff = true\n        connectTimeout = 30 sec\n        smtpServer = localhost\n        threadPoolSize = 2\n\nTheir `secure.config.erb` contains no `smtpPass` — again local MTA, unauthenticated.\n— https://github.com/wikimedia/operations-puppet/blob/production/modules/gerrit/templates/gerrit.config.erb\n\n**openfrontier/gerrit image** (the community image that does expose SMTP env vars). Note both behaviors relevant to us — it **disables email when no server is configured**, and routes the password to `secure.config`:\n\n    if [ -z \"${SMTP_SERVER}\" ]; then\n      set_gerrit_config sendemail.enable false\n    else\n      set_gerrit_config sendemail.enable true\n      set_gerrit_config sendemail.smtpServer \"${SMTP_SERVER}\"\n      [ -z \"${SMTP_SERVER_PORT}\" ] || set_gerrit_config sendemail.smtpServerPort \"${SMTP_SERVER_PORT}\"\n      [ -z \"${SMTP_USER}\" ]        || set_gerrit_config sendemail.smtpUser \"${SMTP_USER}\"\n      [ -z \"${SMTP_PASS}\" ]        || set_secure_config sendemail.smtpPass \"${SMTP_PASS}\"\n      [ -z \"${SMTP_ENCRYPTION}\" ]  || set_gerrit_config sendemail.smtpEncryption \"${SMTP_ENCRYPTION}\"\n    fi\n\n— https://github.com/openfrontier/docker-gerrit/blob/master/gerrit-entrypoint.sh\n\n**Explicitly NOT found** (stated rather than guessed):\n- No public LF Releng / ONAP / O-RAN-SC Gerrit `sendemail` config. The only `org:lfit` hit is `lfit/korg-docs` `source/mail.rst`, which is `git send-email` *client* config for kernel.org — NOT a Gerrit server config. Do not cite it as one.\n- `openstack-infra/puppet-gerrit` has been emptied; no puppet-era template retrieved.\n- `GerritForge/docker-gerrit` is abandoned (CentOS 7.3, Gerrit 2.14) and documents no SMTP env vars.\n- **No actively-maintained large Gerrit deployment was found publishing a `[sendemail]` block pointed at Amazon SES.** The SES config in Option A is assembled from documented Gerrit key names plus AWS SES docs — it is not copied from a production deployment.\n\n## Option A — wire up SES\n\n`etc/gerrit.config`:\n\n    [sendemail]\n        enable = true\n        smtpServer = email-smtp.us-east-1.amazonaws.com\n        smtpServerPort = 587\n        smtpEncryption = tls\n        smtpUser = AKIA................\n        sslVerify = true\n        connectTimeout = 30 sec\n        from = MIXED\n\n`etc/secure.config` (mode 0600, owned by the gerrit user):\n\n    [sendemail]\n        smtpPass = <SES SMTP password>\n\nRestart Gerrit — `sendemail` is not hot-reloadable.\n\n**SES prerequisites (each a hard blocker):**\n\n1. **Sandbox is the #1 gotcha.** New accounts are in the SES sandbox **per Region**: send only to *verified* addresses, max 200 msgs/24h, 1 msg/sec. Reviewer notifications to arbitrary addresses are rejected until production access is granted (console, or `aws sesv2 put-account-details --production-access-enabled --mail-type TRANSACTIONAL`; first Support response ~24h). — https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ses/latest/dg/request-production-access.html\n2. **Verify the sender identity in us-east-1.** Domain identity (Easy DKIM, 3 CNAMEs, up to 72h propagation) allows any address on the domain; an email identity covers one address. Identities are per-Region. Whatever lands in the `From` header must be covered. — https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ses/latest/dg/creating-identities.html\n3. **SMTP credentials are NOT IAM access keys.** \"Your SMTP credentials aren't identical to your AWS access keys.\" Generate via SES console -> SMTP settings -> Create SMTP credentials (creates an IAM user; password shown once). Per-Region. A documented HMAC-SHA256 derivation from an IAM secret key exists, with reference Python in the doc. — https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ses/latest/dg/smtp-credentials.html\n4. **Port 25 is blocked on EC2 by default** — outbound port 25 to public IPv4/IPv6 is blocked. Use 587 (needs no support ticket). — https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/ec2-resource-limits.html#port-25-throttle\n5. **SMTP vs API.** Gerrit's sender is a plain SMTP client with no AWS SDK path, so the SES API is unreachable from Gerrit without an external relay. SMTP it is.\n\n**IAM-role-based auth is NOT possible for SMTP** — directly relevant to our instance-role/SSM pattern:\n\n> \"Don't use temporary AWS credentials to derive SMTP credentials. The SES SMTP interface doesn't support SMTP credentials that have been generated from temporary security credentials.\"\n> — https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ses/latest/dg/smtp-credentials.html\n\nAn EC2 instance profile yields exactly those temporary credentials, so the SMTP interface **requires a long-lived IAM user access key**. A VPC interface endpoint for SMTP exists (ports 465/587/2465/2587) but does not remove the username/password requirement, and is not supported in us-east-1 AZs `use1-az2`, `use1-az3`, `use1-az5`. — https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ses/latest/dg/send-email-set-up-vpc-endpoints.html\n\n**On the SSM SecureString idea (ours):** viable and sensible, but note the limit. AWS's SES guide does not prescribe SSM/Secrets Manager for an app's SMTP password; the nearest AWS precedents are AMS storing SES SMTP credentials in Secrets Manager, and SES Mail Manager referencing relay creds by `secretARN`. Workable shape: keep the SMTP password in SSM SecureString, grant the instance role read access, materialize `etc/secure.config` at boot (same pattern as `ci-gerrit-ssh-key` / `g2p-github-pat`). That gets role-scoped *access to the secret*; the SMTP auth itself stays static. Rotation = new IAM access key + re-derive.\n\n**Historical SES gotcha — now resolved.** An old thread reported SES rejecting Gerrit mail with `554 Transaction failed: Illegal header 'Mailing-List'`. No longer applies: current `MailHeader.java` has no `Mailing-List` header and all custom headers are `X-`-prefixed; SES also broadened header support in 2024.\n\n## Option B — disable cleanly\n\nThis is a first-class, documented option, and precisely the scenario the flag was added for:\n\n> \"If false Gerrit will not send email messages, for any reason, and all other properties of section sendemail are ignored. By default, true, allowing notifications to be sent.\"\n> \"Some installations might be unable to connect to a SMTP relay, but are still useful through the web page UI, provided that reviewers check their dashboard periodically.\"\n> — https://gerrit-review.googlesource.com/Documentation/config-gerrit.html#sendemail\n> — https://opendev.org/opendev/gerrit/commit/2e4573b46409fc7481dbbf64b394bbc2e1ccff27\n\nConfig:\n\n    [sendemail]\n        enable = false\n\nRestart Gerrit. Errors stop immediately — no other `sendemail` key is consulted.\n\n**What is lost:**\n- All notifications: review/comment/merge emails, `user-notify` watches, project watches, abandon/restore, and the review-bot's own mail. Reviewers must use the dashboard.\n- **Self-service email registration/verification breaks.** Registering a new or secondary address sends a verification link; with mail off that flow fails (today it already fails with the same `EmailException`).\n  - Documented admin workaround: `PUT /accounts/{id}/emails/{email}` with `no_confirmation: true`. \"Only Gerrit administrators are allowed to add email addresses without confirmation.\" — https://gerrit-review.googlesource.com/Documentation/rest-api-accounts.html#create-email\n- **Not affected:** git push/fetch, the review UI, SSH/HTTP API, CI integration, label voting, submit, replication, or inbound mail (`[receiveemail]` is a separate section).\n\n## Recommendation\n\n**Set `sendemail.enable = false` now; revisit SES only if a human ever needs out-of-band notification.**\n\n1. It is the documented, upstream-intended remedy for exactly our situation — an installation that cannot reach an SMTP relay but is useful through the UI/API. One line, stops all 4,119 errors immediately, trivially reversible.\n2. It matches how a mainstream Gerrit container already behaves: openfrontier's image sets `sendemail.enable false` automatically when no SMTP server is given.\n3. The functional loss is scoped to notifications plus self-service email verification, and the latter has an official admin bypass (`no_confirmation`). For our bot-driven flow that is close to zero cost.\n4. Option A is not a config change, it is a project: sandbox exit (AWS Support round-trip), domain verification with DNS + DKIM propagation up to 72h, and a long-lived IAM user access key that **cannot** be replaced by our instance role. That is a lot of standing credential surface for mail nobody reads.\n5. Do not attempt to \"fix\" `localhost:25` in place — the official image demonstrably has no MTA.\n\n**If mail is later needed**, two credible paths:\n- **Direct to SES** — Option A config above, port 587 / `tls`. Simplest; couples Gerrit to a static SES credential.\n- **Local relay -> SES** — host-side or sidecar Postfix/msmtp satellite with Gerrit at `smtpServer = localhost`. This is what both OpenDev and Wikimedia actually do (with direct-delivery exim rather than SES), and is the workaround the SES reporter on repo-discuss settled on after failing to connect Gerrit to SES directly. Requires `network_mode: host` or a compose sidecar since our container has no MTA. Buys queueing/retry and keeps the SES credential out of Gerrit's config; costs another moving part.\n- A third-party (SendGrid/Mailgun) is configuration-identical from Gerrit's side and skips SES's sandbox and identity verification, at the cost of another vendor. No Gerrit-specific documentation was found for these — that is inference from the shared SMTP key set, not a citation."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785451744250060001,"uuid":"ad76062f-c68c-4852-a71b-05aed49be585"}